{"name":"Tawny Owl","hex":"#978b71","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tawny owl","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any member of the species Strix aluco of strigid owls, of Europe, western Asia, and the Middle East."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tawny_owl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tawny owl","blocks":[{"text":"The tawny owl, also called the brown owl, is a stocky, medium-sized owl in the family Strigidae. It is commonly found in woodlands across Europe, as well as western Siberia, and has seven recognized subspecies. The tawny owl's underparts are pale with dark streaks, whilst its upper body may be either brown or grey. The tawny owl typically makes its nest in a tree hole where it can protect its eggs and young against potential predators. It is non-migratory and highly territorial: as a result, when young birds grow up and leave the parental nest, if they cannot find a vacant territory to claim as their own, they will often starve."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Strix_aluco_3_%28Martin_Mecnarowski%29.jpg/330px-Strix_aluco_3_%28Martin_Mecnarowski%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tawny_owl"},{"title":"Owl","blocks":[{"text":"Owls are birds from the order Strigiformes, which includes over 200 species of mostly solitary and nocturnal birds of prey typified by an upright stance, a large, broad head, binocular vision, binaural hearing, sharp talons, and feathers adapted for silent flight. Exceptions include the diurnal northern hawk-owl and the gregarious burrowing owl."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Bubo_bubo_sibiricus_-_01.JPG/330px-Bubo_bubo_sibiricus_-_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Owl"},{"title":"Tawny (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Tawny is a light brown to brownish-orange colour."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Natugle_stor.jpg/330px-Natugle_stor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tawny_(colour)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Poll_de_gamar%C3%BAs_eurasi%C3%A0tic_%28Strix_aluco%29.jpg/500px-Poll_de_gamar%C3%BAs_eurasi%C3%A0tic_%28Strix_aluco%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Poll_de_gamar%C3%BAs_eurasi%C3%A0tic_%28Strix_aluco%29.jpg/1280px-Poll_de_gamar%C3%BAs_eurasi%C3%A0tic_%28Strix_aluco%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Poll_de_gamar%C3%BAs_eurasi%C3%A0tic_%28Strix_aluco%29.jpg/500px-Poll_de_gamar%C3%BAs_eurasi%C3%A0tic_%28Strix_aluco%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Juvenile specimen of a tawny owl","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APoll_de_gamar%C3%BAs_eurasi%C3%A0tic_(Strix_aluco).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Nicolas_Robert%2C_Le_Chat-huant%2C_MNHN.jpg/500px-Nicolas_Robert%2C_Le_Chat-huant%2C_MNHN.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Nicolas_Robert%2C_Le_Chat-huant%2C_MNHN.jpg/1280px-Nicolas_Robert%2C_Le_Chat-huant%2C_MNHN.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Nicolas_Robert%2C_Le_Chat-huant%2C_MNHN.jpg/500px-Nicolas_Robert%2C_Le_Chat-huant%2C_MNHN.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nicolas Robert, Le Chat-huant, strix aluco, 17th century, National Museum of Natural History, France.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.